\ cv8-save.4 - SAVE-SYSTEM writing a CV8 image.
\
\ The last piece of phase 3: a CV8 system that can save itself, with no
\ cell image and no translator in the loop. Load after save-system.4
\ (and after cv8.4); --cv8-compiler swaps `SAVE-SYSTEM8` into
\ `SAVE-SYSTEM` and `SS-SET-MAGIC8` into `SS-SET-MAGIC`.
\
\ The only real difference from the cell version is the header. A cell
\ image is 8 magic bytes then the dictionary; a CV8 image is
\ (CV8-REFERENCE.md 5.1):
\
\     magic 8 bytes   "CV8" + scale digit, cell width, 'L', version, flags
\     head_nfa  cell  offset of the newest word's name field
\     ntails    cell  DOES> tail count - always 0 here, because DODOES
\                     carries its target inline (--dataprims)
\     locals    5 cells, present whenever the specialised opcodes are
\
\ Everything else - SS-SCRUB, the unrelocation, RESET-BUFFERS - is the
\ cell version's and is already right: it zeroes every cell holding a
\ live-session address, which is exactly what made TRANSLATED images
\ non-reproducible across dumps.

BASE @  DECIMAL


: SS-SET-MAGIC8 ( --- )
  \ Must agree with the engine's IMAGE_MAGIC exactly, including the
  \ feature bits, or the image is refused at load.
  [CHAR] C SS-MAGIC     C!
  [CHAR] V SS-MAGIC 1+  C!
  [CHAR] 8 SS-MAGIC 2 + C!
  \ scale digit: 3 on a 64-bit cell, 2 on a 32-bit one
  48 CV8-SHIFT + SS-MAGIC 3 + C!
  1 CELLS  SS-MAGIC 4 + C!
  [CHAR] L SS-MAGIC 5 + C!      \ specialised opcodes present
  1        SS-MAGIC 6 + C!      \ format version
  15       SS-MAGIC 7 + C! ;    \ varcall | varslot | spec | lit64

: SS-CELL, ( n --- )
  \ Append one cell to the image file, reusing SS-MAGIC as scratch: the
  \ magic itself has already been written by then.
  SS-MAGIC ! SS-MAGIC 1 CELLS SS-FID @ WRITE-FILE DROP ;

CREATE SS-NAME 40 ALLOT
\ A PRIVATE counted-string buffer. NOT NAMEBUF: that is the outer
\ interpreter's scratch, and it is overwritten by the very next word
\ the interpreter looks up - a probe that used it read back "TYPE",
\ the next word of the probe itself.

: SS-FIND ( c-addr u --- xt )
\ Look a word up at RUN time. NOT ['] and NOT ' into a variable: both
\ capture an address of the session that COMPILED this file, which for
\ a translated image is the cell build and is meaningless in the saved
\ image. Executing a word is relocated; a compile-time address is not.
  39 MIN SS-NAME PLACE SS-NAME FIND 0= ABORT" cv8-save: word not found" ;

: SS-PFA ( c-addr u --- offset )
\ Offset of a data word's parameter field: the body is [DOVAR][pad],
\ one cell, then the field.
  SS-FIND 1 CELLS + START @ - ;

: SS-HEADS, ( --- )
  \ The word list, as the engine's loader now wants it: a thread COUNT
  \ followed by that many START-relative heads.
  \
  \ It used to be a single head_nfa, and the loader walked that one
  \ chain to build its word table. Once the dictionary was hashed there
  \ was no single chain to walk - one thread reaches about a thirty-
  \ second of the dictionary - so the header carries every head and the
  \ loader walks them all. SOD16 needs them because it names a call by
  \ word NUMBER; CV8 names a call by address and ignores this entirely,
  \ but the header shape is shared.
  \
  \ Read from the LIVE word list, not the copy: SS-UNRELOCATE-WORDLIST
  \ has already rewritten the copy's heads to their on-disk form, and
  \ these need the same treatment applied to the live values.
  \
  \ DUP, not ?DUP. An empty thread is 0 and must be emitted as 0; ?DUP
  \ leaves nothing on the stack in that case and SS-CELL, would then
  \ write whatever was underneath it.
  FORTH-WORDLIST @ DUP SS-CELL,
  1+ 1 DO
    FORTH-WORDLIST I CELLS + @ DUP IF START @ - THEN SS-CELL,
  LOOP ;

: SS-HEADER, ( --- )
  \ The CV8 header fields that follow the magic.
  SS-HEADS,                                 \ nthreads, then the heads
  0 SS-CELL,                                \ ntails: DODOES is self-contained
  S" LSAVE-SP"    SS-PFA  SS-CELL,          \ the locals save-stack pointer
  S" LSAVE-STACK" SS-PFA  SS-CELL,          \ and its buffer
  LSAVE-MAX               SS-CELL,          \ its limit
  S" LSAVE"       SS-FIND START @ - SS-CELL,   \ fallbacks for ABORT" paths
  S" LRESTORE"    SS-FIND START @ - SS-CELL, ;

: SS-SCRUB-ALL ( --- )   \ NOT "SS-SCRUB8": that name would make --cv8-compiler
\ replace SS-SCRUB itself, and this word calls it - infinite recursion,
\ which showed up as a return stack overflow.
\ SS-SCRUB knows the kernel's, locals' and pool's scratch cells, but
\ not shell.4's. These five hold tokenizer positions from whatever the
\ builder was last parsing; leaving them made two saves of the same
\ system differ (found by comparing gen1 with gen2) and made the saved
\ image start with stale parser state, which failed two test files.
  SS-SCRUB
  \ The image is always saved in the MIDDLE of a command - the one that
  \ called SAVE-SYSTEM - so every parser cursor holds live state. Found
  \ by diffing a saved image against a translated one that passes: the
  \ saved shell reported "if: expected 'then'", i.e. it resumed mid-parse.
  TOK-POS SS-CELL   TOK-END SS-CELL
  TOK-OUT SS-CELL   TOK-OUT-END SS-CELL
  TAP-S SS-CELL     TAP-L SS-CELL      TAP-I SS-CELL
  NORM-WCOUNT SS-CELL   NORM-WSTART SS-CELL
  TOK-WAS-QUOTED? SS-CELL
  ARGC SS-CELL      ARGC2 SS-CELL
  ARGC-SEMI-LEFT SS-CELL   ARGC-AO-LEFT SS-CELL
  EW-ARGC SS-CELL   EW-I SS-CELL
  JC-LEN SS-CELL    JQ-LEN SS-CELL
  FTH-LEN SS-CELL   FTH-I SS-CELL
  PIPE-ARGC SS-CELL PIPE-NSEG SS-CELL
  PP-BAR SS-CELL    PP-I SS-CELL
  RAW-LINE-LEN SS-CELL
  SHFILE-FID SS-CELL   SHFILE-ACTIVE? SS-CELL ;

: SAVE-SYSTEM8 ( c-addr u --- )
  RESET-BUFFERS
  HERE START @ - SS-LEN !
  SS-LEN @ ALLOCATE ABORT" SAVE-SYSTEM: out of memory" SS-BUF !
  START @ SS-BUF @ SS-LEN @ MOVE
  DP SS-UNRELOCATE
  FORTH-WORDLIST SS-UNRELOCATE-WORDLIST
  SS-SCRUB-ALL
  SS-SET-MAGIC
  W/O BIN CREATE-FILE ABORT" SAVE-SYSTEM: cannot create file" SS-FID !
  SS-MAGIC 8 SS-FID @ WRITE-FILE DROP
  SS-HEADER,
  SS-BUF @ SS-LEN @ SS-FID @ WRITE-FILE DROP
  SS-FID @ CLOSE-FILE DROP
  SS-BUF @ FREE DROP ;

BASE !
